# 6. Figure 1 到 Figure 7 的图注草案

## Figure 1

**Teaser: GUI agents know where to click before coordinate serialization.**
Intermediate layers produce a sharp spatial posterior around the target widget, while final layers may become less spatially localized. We hypothesize that late layers focus on serializing the spatial intent into coordinate/action tokens rather than preserving a clean screen-space posterior.

## Figure 2

**Layerwise spatial lens fingerprint.**
Grounding quality is highly non-monotonic across depth. Qwen2.5-VL based agents peak around L20–L22, while Qwen3-VL based agents peak later and exhibit a wider spatial plateau. Final layers are not consistently the best spatial decoders.

## Figure 3

**Spatial localization peaks before coordinate/action serialization.**
The spatial lens decodes target regions most accurately from intermediate layers, whereas the serialization lens predicts coordinate/action protocol tokens better in later layers. This peak mismatch supports the coordinate serialization bottleneck hypothesis.

## Figure 4

**Layer aggregation hides the best grounding layer.**
All-layer query attention produces diffuse candidate regions, while the best intermediate spatial probe gives a sharper target posterior. The relevant signal is not merely prefill attention, but the specific layer where spatial grounding becomes decodable.

## Figure 5

**The spatial posterior follows the instruction.**
On the same screenshot, changing only the instruction shifts the mid-layer posterior from one UI element to another. This suggests that the probe captures instruction-conditioned grounding rather than generic UI saliency.

## Figure 6

**High-resolution small targets amplify the bottleneck.**
The gap between best intermediate layers and final layers grows for smaller targets and harder layouts. The large overlap-hit gap indicates that models often identify the right region but lose precision during patch-to-coordinate serialization.

## Figure 7

**Layerwise hit@1 across SS-Pro and OSWorld-G.**
Across four GUI agents, hit@1 peaks before the final layer. The effect is strongest on SS-Pro, where high-resolution and dense layouts make coordinate serialization especially brittle.

